============================================================
PYTHON PATHS
Understanding How Python Finds Files
============================================================

1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 Understanding Python Paths

Understanding paths is one of the most confusing topics when starting with Python.

When working with Python projects, you will frequently encounter errors related to:

- File paths.
- Module imports.
- Folder navigation.
- Missing files.
- Incorrect directory references.

These errors are common when working with multiple Python files and folders.

It is completely normal to make mistakes while learning how paths work.

The best way to understand paths is through practice and by learning how Python locates files and modules.

1.2 Why Are Python Paths Important?

Python paths help you locate and access files and modules within your project.

For example:

- Reading a CSV file.
- Loading configuration files.
- Importing Python modules.
- Accessing files inside subdirectories.
- Importing code from parent directories.

Understanding paths becomes especially important when working with larger Python projects, AI applications, and Machine Learning projects.

============================================================
2. THE MENTAL MODEL
============================================================

2.1 Two Important Questions

Whenever you work with multiple files and folders, always think about two things:

1. Where Am I?

   Identify the current location of your Python script or the current working directory of your program.

2. Where Do I Want to Go?

   Identify the file or Python module you want to access.

Once you understand these two things, navigating between files and folders becomes much easier.

2.2 Understanding Folder Navigation

There are different ways to navigate between folders depending on whether you are accessing files or importing Python modules.

A. Going Down Into Subfolders

When accessing files:

Use "/" to move into subdirectories.

Example:

    data/input.csv

This refers to the "input.csv" file inside the "data" folder.

When importing Python modules:

Use "." to represent folder or package navigation.

Example:

    from data.preprocessing import clean_data

Here:
- data is the parent package.
- preprocessing is the module.
- clean_data is the function being imported.

Note:
The required package structure and Python import configuration must be in place.

B. Going Up to Parent Folders

When accessing files:

Use "../" to move one directory level up.

Example:

    ../data/input.csv

This means:
- Go up one directory.
- Enter the "data" folder.
- Access "input.csv".

When importing Python modules:

You generally cannot use "../" in a normal import statement.

Instead, you may need to configure the Python module search path using sys.path or organize your project as a proper package.

Example:

    import sys
    sys.path.append("..")

Note:
The path added to sys.path should point to the directory containing the module or package you want to import.

C. Accessing Files in the Same Folder

When the required file is in the same directory, you can directly use its filename.

Example:

    input.csv

For importing a module in the same package:

    import preprocessing

Or:

    from preprocessing import clean_data

Note:
Python imports depend on the package structure and module search path.

